From: Veron <veron@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] init_putv: a narrow host long double must be CONVERTED, not copied

WHAT 0005 GOT RIGHT AND WHAT IT GOT WRONG. Patch 0005 added the missing branch
for a host whose long double is smaller than the target's:

    else if (sizeof(long double) < LDOUBLE_SIZE)
        memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, sizeof(long double));

The branch belongs there. What it does is wrong. x86_64-tcc's own long double
is eight bytes -- an IEEE double, because mc-tcc compiled it and patch 0001
sets LDOUBLE_SIZE 8 for that reason -- while the x86_64 target's long double
is x87 80-bit extended. Those are different REPRESENTATIONS, not just different
widths. Copying the eight bytes leaves a double sitting in a long double's
slot, and every later `fldt` reads its bits as an 80-bit mantissa and exponent.

WHERE IT BITES. musl's src/internal/floatscan.c accumulates decimal digits with

    y = 1000000000.0L * y + x[a+i & MASK];

x86_64-tcc emits that long double constant as

    00 00 00 00 65 cd cd 41              the double 1e9, upper half left as-is

where the target needs

    00 00 00 00 00 28 6b ee 1c 40        1e9 as x87 extended

so decfloat multiplies by garbage. Small integers survive because decfloat
returns them from its fast path -- `if (lrp==dc && dc<10 ...) return sign *
(long double)x[0]` -- without touching the constant, which is why 1.0, 3.0 and
1024.0 look fine while 0.5, 0.1 and 123456789.0 do not.

THE CHAIN THAT FOLLOWS. tcc built against that musl parses every decimal float
literal wrongly. gcc's real.c carries

    #define M_LOG10_2 0.30102999566398119521

and a gcc built by such a tcc holds 1.266209359584991e-12 instead, so cc1 dies
at real.c:1724 on the first float constant it is asked to print. That is what
`cc1 -E -dM /dev/null` does, which is what configure does, which is where rung
6 of the hermetic ladder stops.

VERIFIED END TO END, LOCALLY, FROM STAGE 0. The whole seed was rebuilt from the
vendored sources: micro-c (M2-Planet plus the 76-patch series), M1 and hex2
with the max_string raise the seed makes, then mc-tcc -- whose .M1 part sizes
match the seed log exactly at 20750 and 14772 lines -- then an aarch64 musl
with rungs.sh's bits/float.h rewrite, then x86_64-tcc, then an x86_64 musl,
then tcc-x86_64. Twice, differing only in whether this patch was applied:

    without 0007   tcc-x86_64 gives 0.5 as 0x1.9796cp-35   -- and that is
                   000000006c79c93d, byte for byte what the shipped
                   tcc-x86_64 produces, so the reproduction is faithful
    with 0007      0.5, 1.5, 0.1, 0.30102999566398119521, 123456789.0 and
                   1e300 all exactly correct, M_LOG10_2 included

BOTH HALVES ARE NEEDED, which is why an earlier attempt looked like a failure.
A fixed x86_64-tcc linked against a musl built by the UNFIXED one still gives
the wrong answers: the broken floatscan is already baked into that libc. The
correction only lands once the fixed compiler builds the libc as well, which is
what the ladder does anyway.

THE CONVERSION IS INTEGER ONLY, on purpose. This code runs inside a compiler
that mc-tcc built, and micro-c maps float, double and long double to one
word-sized type -- patch 0004 exists because the constant folder could not do
float arithmetic there either. So the bit pattern is taken apart and
reassembled with shifts and masks, never with a floating cast.

    double   sign | 11-bit exponent, bias 1023  | 52-bit mantissa, implicit 1
    x87      sign | 15-bit exponent, bias 16383 | 64-bit mantissa, EXPLICIT 1

so the exponent is rebiased, the mantissa is shifted left eleven places, and
the integer bit is set explicitly. Zero, infinity, NaN and subnormals are each
handled: a subnormal double has no implicit 1 and is normalised into the
extended range, which is representable there because the exponent is far wider.

The conversion was also checked directly against `(long double)v` on x86_64 for
2^96, 2^64, 2^32, 0.5, 1.5, 0.0, -2.0, 1e300 and 0.1 -- all ten bytes identical
in every case.

WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Rungs 6 and beyond have not been run with this patch. What
is shown is that the compiler which was producing a broken gcc now produces
correct floating-point constants, which is the thing that was breaking it.

--- a/tccgen.c
+++ b/tccgen.c
@@ -8074,8 +8074,49 @@
 #endif
                 else if (sizeof(double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
                     *(double*)ptr = (double)vtop->c.ld;
-                else if (sizeof(long double) < LDOUBLE_SIZE)
-                    memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, sizeof(long double));
+                else if (sizeof(long double) < LDOUBLE_SIZE) {
+                    /* THE HOST'S long double IS AN IEEE DOUBLE; THE TARGET'S
+                       IS x87 80-BIT EXTENDED. Different representations, not
+                       just different widths -- convert, do not copy. Patch
+                       0005 copied, which left a double sitting in a long
+                       double's slot and every later fldt reading its bits as
+                       an 80-bit mantissa and exponent.
+
+                       INTEGER ONLY, because micro-c gives this compiler no
+                       float arithmetic -- the same reason patch 0004 exists.
+
+                         double  sign | 11-bit exp bias 1023  | 52-bit mant, implicit 1
+                         x87     sign | 15-bit exp bias 16383 | 64-bit mant, EXPLICIT 1 */
+                    unsigned char *q; unsigned long long b, m; int e, i, s;
+                    q = (unsigned char *)ptr;
+                    b = 0;
+                    memcpy(&b, &vtop->c.ld, 8);
+                    for (i = 0; i < LDOUBLE_SIZE; i++)
+                        q[i] = 0;
+                    s = (int)(b >> 63);
+                    e = (int)((b >> 52) & 0x7ff);
+                    m = b & 0xfffffffffffffULL;
+                    if (e == 0 && m == 0) {
+                        e = 0; m = 0;
+                    } else if (e == 0x7ff) {
+                        e = 0x7fff;
+                        m = (m << 11) | 0x8000000000000000ULL;
+                    } else if (e == 0) {
+                        e = 1 - 1023 + 16383;
+                        while (!(m & 0x10000000000000ULL)) {
+                            m = m << 1;
+                            e = e - 1;
+                        }
+                        m = m << 11;
+                    } else {
+                        e = e - 1023 + 16383;
+                        m = (m << 11) | 0x8000000000000000ULL;
+                    }
+                    for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+                        q[i] = (unsigned char)((m >> (i * 8)) & 0xff);
+                    q[8] = (unsigned char)(e & 0xff);
+                    q[9] = (unsigned char)(((e >> 8) & 0x7f) | (s << 7));
+                }
                 else if (0 == memcmp(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE))
                     ; /* nothing to do for 0.0 */
 #ifndef TCC_CROSS_TEST
